</div>
<div class="dinolab-3d-shell">
<div class="dinolab-3d-viewer" style="position:relative;min-height:clamp(520px, 62vh, 760px);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden;border:1px solid rgba(148,163,184,0.26);background:#0f172a">
<div id="dino3d-viewer-desc-tyrannosaurus" style="position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;padding:0;margin:-1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0, 0, 0, 0);white-space:nowrap;border:0">T. rex 3D model summary. Visible layers: skeleton proxy, body outline, human scale, evidence markers. Environment scene hypothesis: Seasonal floodplain / open woodland. A broad environmental scene hypothesis based on the listed formation and region; vegetation, water, and weather remain interpretive. Pose cue: Balanced bipedal stance with lightly flexed knees. Foot contact and joint flex are clade-informed visual cues; exact habitual posture remains inferred. Color evidence: Inferred palette. No direct pigment map is encoded for this taxon; the color is an evidence-compatible illustration, not a measured reconstruction. Reconstruction profile: Deep-skulled large theropod with strong fossil coverage. Active surface hypothesis: Evidence-led reconstruction, status Recommended. Unpreserved body regions remain uncertain; direct evidence from one patch does not automatically describe the whole animal. Limb osteology: Tapered long-bone shafts, expanded articular ends, and separate paired forearm and shank bones replace uniform rod proxies.; evidence basis: Shaft proportions and major processes follow the represented clade; exact curvature, torsion, and joint cartilage are not recoverable from a generic reconstruction.. Girdle architecture: Blade-like scapulae and ilia frame distinct shoulder and hip sockets; the pubis and ischium remain separate directional struts.. Vertebral architecture: Oriented centra carry neural arches, neural spines, restrained articular-facet proxies, and region-specific transverse processes.; evidence basis: Centrum proportions and preserved processes constrain the axial column; intervertebral cartilage, ligament thickness, and the exact neutral pose remain inferred.. Thoracic cage: Double-headed dorsal ribs separate the capitulum and tuberculum, then curve through a tapered costal shaft toward the ventral thorax.; evidence basis: Preserved proximal rib heads constrain vertebral articulation and shaft curvature; distal cartilage, exact rib spacing, and resting thoracic volume remain inferred.. Gastral basket: paired, imbricated gastral basket with separate medial and lateral segments. Uncinate evidence: No ossified uncinate processes are generalized for this profile; unpreserved cartilaginous processes cannot be ruled out.. Sacral load path: 5-element series; Compact sacral centra carry neural arches and broad paired sacral ribs that expand laterally into load-bearing contacts against the ilia.; evidence basis: Sacral-rib morphology and iliac attachment facets identify the load path; co-ossification sequence, cartilage thickness, and element boundaries vary with taxon and maturity.. Caudal transition: Large proximal caudal ribs and neural spines diminish gradually toward a transition region rather than disappearing at one identical vertebra in every dinosaur.. Haemal arches: Each rendered chevron uses paired proximal rami around a haemal canal that merge into a tapering ventral haemal spine.; evidence basis: Articular facets and preserved haemal arches constrain proximal attachment and broad form; exact spacing, distal outline, and the last chevron-bearing vertebra vary among taxa.. Distal limbs: Digitigrade metatarsus with unequal articulated phalanges and narrow terminal unguals.; evidence basis: Digit counts are clade constrained, while exact phalangeal formulae, cartilage, interdigital tissue, and resting spread require specimen-level evidence.. Postcranial surface silhouette: Deep chest, narrow waist, powerful pelvis, and muscular tail base. Overlapping trunk volumes and graded neck, limb, and tail transitions follow clade-level postcranial proportions. Muscle thickness, skin folds, and the exact resting contour remain inferred. Cranial surface silhouette: Deep, broad tyrannosaur cranial silhouette. This visible head envelope follows clade-level skull proportions; lips, cheeks, brow thickness, beak margins, and external nostril shape remain inferred. Cranial mechanics: braced, predominantly akinetic non-avian theropod skull; evidence basis: Most non-avian theropod quadrates were not freely streptostylic; a synovial upper contact alone does not establish bird-like cranial kinesis.. Head-neck junction: A single occipital condyle meets a ring-like atlas assembled from paired neural arches and a ventral intercentrum; the axis carries an odontoid proxy and a taller neural spine.; evidence basis: The bony joint surfaces and atlas-axis elements constrain the head-neck connection. Cartilage thickness, ligament paths, fusion state, and maximum head excursion remain specimen- and maturity-dependent.. Mandibular lever: The coronoid process, surangular, articular, and retroarticular process form a clade-scaled mandibular lever around the quadrate hinge.; evidence basis: Coronoid height, the articular glenoid, and retroarticular length are osteological constraints on lever geometry; muscle mass, fiber orientation, bite force, and soft joint tissues are inferred.. Integument mode: scaled; evidence basis: direct scale impressions in large derived tyrannosaurids. Respiratory reconstruction: air-sac proxy; evidence basis: Vertebral fossae, foramina, and internal chambers are osteological correlates of air-filled diverticula; their distribution varies among theropod lineages.. Current scan focus: Skull. Logged anchors: 0 of 3. Evidence path: 0 of 2. Assembly progress: 0 of 6. Keyboard controls: Left and Right Arrow or A and D rotate; Up and Down Arrow raise or lower the camera; Page Up and Page Down zoom; Home resets the view.</div>
<canvas id="dinolab-3d-canvas-tyrannosaurus" class="dinolab-3d-canvas" tabindex="0" role="application" aria-roledescription="Interactive 3D dinosaur reconstruction" aria-keyshortcuts="ArrowLeft ArrowRight ArrowUp ArrowDown PageUp PageDown A D Home" aria-describedby="dino3d-viewer-desc-tyrannosaurus dino3d-status-tyrannosaurus" aria-label="T. rex procedural 3D reconstruction viewer. Environment scene hypothesis: Floodplain woodland. Drag in two directions to orbit and use the wheel to zoom. Arrow keys rotate and raise or lower the camera; Page Up and Page Down zoom; Home resets the view." style="width:100%;height:clamp(520px, 62vh, 760px);display:block;touch-action:none;outline:none;outline-offset:-3px">
</canvas>
